Done and committed as 0fd18821. cargo build, cargo test (all 31 test binaries), clippy -D warnings and fmt --check all pass.

What was wrong: positional_after_verb in src/dispatch_sub.rs treated any word starting with - as a flag without checking it was a real one. So yoyo init --froce exited 0 and wrote YOYO.md, and status, version, doctor, evolution, help, security and permissions silently ignored --bogus. (health ignored it too and exited 1 for its usual "no project" reason.)

The fix: that one function now checks every word before -- against KNOWN_FLAGS. An unknown flag exits 2 before the command runs, and the error names the command and suggests a close real flag:
error: unknown flag '--verbos' (did you mean --verbose?) for \yoyo status\. Nothing ran.
That covers all 10 commands that use the function: doctor, health, help, version, init, security, update, status, evolution and permissions. The suggestion logic isn't a copy: I split the existing top-level refusal (#1109) into a shared cli_config::unknown_flags_in, which both places now call. The #1109 tests weren't changed and still pass.

Census: I ran <verb> --bogus for 38 of the 39 commands in KNOWN_SUBCOMMANDS, using the built binary in a fresh temp repo. I skipped update beforehand because it would really try to self-update; after the fix I ran it by hand, and it refuses before any network call.
• Before: 12 commands exited 0.
• After: 4 still exit 0. def, find, memories and outline take --bogus as the thing to search for rather than ignoring it, so they're a different class and not in this slice.

Tests (4 new ones in tests/unknown_flag_refusal.rs, using that file's existing helper that strips API keys from the environment):
• init --froce exits 2 and YOYO.md does not exist afterwards. As a check that the test can fail, plain init in the same kind of directory does write it.
• All 8 commands from the census that ignored --bogus now exit 2 with nothing on stdout.
• The did-you-mean suggestion appears.
• These keep their current behaviour: status, status --no-color, status --model foo (foo is still taken as the flag's value), version -v/--verbose, and status stray, which still gets the old "unexpected argument" message with exit 2.

As a control, I disabled the new check, ran the tests and put it back in one command. Exactly the three refusal tests failed, the behaviour-preserving test stayed green, and everything passed again once it was restored.

I didn't add the Codex-style hint about using -- before a value that starts with -, because none of these commands takes such a value.

Docs: docs/src/usage/commands.md now says these commands refuse an unknown flag with exit 2.

Issue #1111: I posted one comment ([link](https://github.com/yologdev/yoyo-evolve/issues/1111#issuecomment-6563513609)). It opens with what's still open and covers the census numbers, the 4 search commands left for a separate decision, and the commands that exit 1 for other reasons. I didn't check whether exit 1 is the right code for a flag typo in each of those.
